{"version":"ai-mcq-bank-v2-la-batch15-high-quality-rewritten-affected-2026-05-22-stem-fixed-inequality-rendering-fixed-student-facing-qc-2026-05-31-pt-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch2-foundations-counting-2026-06-11-pt-batch3-counting-probability-rules-2026-06-11-pt-batch4-conditioning-2026-06-11-pt-batch5-random-variables-2026-06-11-pt-batch6-discrete-distributions-2026-06-11-pt-batch7-distribution-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch8-continuous-expectation-2026-06-11-pt-batch9-non-week7-expectation-transform-2026-06-11-pt-batch10-non-week7-process-summary-2026-06-11-pt-batch11-non-week7-final-summary-shape-2026-06-11-quality-round1-2026-06-14-quality-round2-2026-06-14-quality-round3-2026-06-14-quality-round4-deep-2026-06-14+quality-round5-difficulty-2026-06-14+quality-round6-expression-2026-06-14+quality-round7-answer-cue-2026-06-14+quality-round8-answer-shape-2026-06-14+quality-round9-answer-position-2026-06-14+quality-round10-template-padding-2026-06-14+quality-round11-generic-stems-2026-06-14+quality-round12-deterministic-cleanup-2026-06-15+quality-pilot-deep-rework-2026-06-15+v3-2026-10-04+upright-2026-10-04+v3-2026-10-04+v3-2026-10-06+v3-2026-10-06+qa-fixes-2026-10-08","concept_id":"Year-1/2b-Probability-Distributions/joint-cdf-continuous.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/2b-Probability-Distributions/joint-cdf-continuous.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a pair with joint density $f_{X,Y}$, which formula gives the joint CDF $F_{X,Y}(x,y)$?","options":["$f_{X,Y}(x,y)$","$\\int_x^\\infty\\int_y^\\infty f_{X,Y}(u,v)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u$","$\\int_{-\\infty}^x\\int_{-\\infty}^yf_{X,Y}(u,v)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u$","$F_X(x)\\,F_Y(y)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d262b44a87a013c1e231637b","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A pair has continuous joint density $f_{X,Y}$ supported on $[0,1]^2$ and joint CDF $F$. For $0<x,y<1$, a student differentiates $F(x,y)$ once with respect to $x$. Which expression results?","options":["$\\int_0^y f_{X,Y}(x,v)\\,\\mathrm{d}v$","$f_{X,Y}(x,y)$","$\\int_0^x f_{X,Y}(u,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}u$","$\\int_0^1 f_{X,Y}(x,v)\\,\\mathrm{d}v$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c8013064c6abe8832b15663c","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For $a<b$ and $c<d$, a proposed joint CDF $H$ has $H(a,c)=0.1$, $H(a,d)=0.4$, $H(b,c)=0.4$ and $H(b,d)=0.6$. What do these values imply?","options":["Its rectangle probability $\\mathbb{P}(a<X\\le b,\\,c<Y\\le d)$ is $0.5$","The rectangle $(a,b]\\times(c,d]$ has probability $0.2$.","The four values satisfy the necessary rectangle condition.","The four values violate the necessary rectangle condition."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1ee2b4ba354f8b9cc4ae462b","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which random pairs have a joint CDF?","options":["Only pairs with a joint density","Every random pair","Only pairs of discrete variables","Only independent pairs"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"35365256473ce4874e81b787","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $X$ be continuous and $Y=X$. What is $F_{X,Y}(x,y)$?","options":["$F_X(x)\\,F_X(y)$","$F_X(\\max(x,y))$","$F_X(\\min(x,y))$","$F_X(x)+F_X(y)-1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c6cc68e5dc614f661c4cebea","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"$X$ and $Y$ are independent $\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$. What is $F_{X,Y}(0.3,0.6)$?","options":["$0.9$","$0.18$","$0.3$","$0.72$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"07bdd691162fe2d262adb1ac","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For a pair with a joint density, $F_X(2)=0.6$, $F_Y(3)=0.7$ and $F_{X,Y}(2,3)=0.45$. What is $\\mathbb{P}(X>2,Y>3)$?","options":["$0.55$","$0.12$","$0.30$","$0.15$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"23f0b32f812357faf9091241","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"On the unit square a pair has joint CDF $F(x,y)=x^2y$. What is its joint density there?","options":["$2x$","$x^2$","$2xy$","$x^2y$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b0b416b92d806206fba754f6","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"$(X,Y)$ is uniform on the unit square, so $F(x,y)=xy$ there. What is $\\mathbb{P}(0.2<X\\le0.5,\\ 0.1<Y\\le0.4)$?","options":["$0.18$","$0.07$","$0.09$","$0.2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9820f8f08ab67206b1c2e3fe","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A pair has joint CDF $F(x,y)=(1-\\mathrm{e}^{-x})(1-\\mathrm{e}^{-2y})$ for $x,y\\ge0$, and $F(x,y)=0$ if either coordinate is negative. For $x\\ge0$, what is the marginal CDF $F_X(x)$?","options":["$(1-\\mathrm{e}^{-x})(1-\\mathrm{e}^{-2x})$","$1-\\mathrm{e}^{-x}$","$1-\\mathrm{e}^{-2x}$","$1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7d36e943c4214063e81c6190","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A random pair has joint CDF $F$ and $F_Y(2)=0.65$. A student claims that $F(n,2)\\to1$ as the positive integer $n$ increases because the threshold for $X$ tends to infinity. Which assessment is correct?","options":["The limit is $1$.","The limit is $0$.","The limit is $0.65$ only if the variables are independent.","The limit is $0.65$ for every dependence structure."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"612827af25aa0eb4e5e93783","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a pair with a joint density, how does $\\mathbb{P}(X\\le x,\\ Y<y)$ relate to $F_{X,Y}(x,y)$?","options":["It equals $F_{X,Y}(x,y)$.","It equals $F_{X,Y}(x,y)-f_{X,Y}(x,y)$.","It equals $F_X(x)\\,F_Y(y)$.","It is strictly smaller than $F_{X,Y}(x,y)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9b8600bd27e09aaf89c1c81b","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"$(X,Y)$ has joint CDF $F$. What is the joint CDF of $(2X,2Y)$ at $(x,y)$?","options":["$\\tfrac14F(x,y)$","$F(2x,2y)$","$F(\\tfrac x2,\\tfrac y2)$","$F(\\tfrac x2,y)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"14f336bc80799701636e1e14","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A pair has joint density $x+y$ on the unit square. What is $F_{X,Y}(\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12)$?","options":["$\\tfrac18$","$\\tfrac14$","$\\tfrac9{64}$","$\\tfrac38$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"45229ed14aa8c2ce3794d3fb","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"$(X,Y)$ is uniform on the triangle $0<y<x<1$. Using the joint CDF, what is $\\mathbb{P}(\\max(X,Y)\\le t)$ for $0<t<1$?","options":["$t$","$t^2$","$t^2(2t-t^2)$","$2t-t^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9d6d7d20142a1a75438a9ceb","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Suppose $F_X(0.5)=0.4$ and $F_{X,Y}(0.5,1)=0.3$. What is $\\mathbb{P}(X\\le0.5,\\ Y>1)$?","options":["$0.7$","$0.3$","$0.12$","$0.1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"42a50f5fa72c215856412834","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which pair has joint CDF $F(x,y)=\\max(0,\\,x+y-1)$ on $[0,1]^2$?","options":["$X\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$ and $Y=1-X$","Independent $\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$ variables","$X\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$ and $Y=X$","A uniform point in $\\{(x,y):0<x<1,\\ 0<y<1,\\ x+y>1\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6ce551e6b427f17d4487a938","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For $Y=X\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$, the joint CDF is $F(x,y)=\\min(x,y)$ on $[0,1]^2$. Which statement correctly describes its mixed derivative and the existence of a joint density?","options":["It equals $1$, so the pair is uniform on the square","It is $0$ off the diagonal, so no joint density exists","It equals $\\min(x,y)$, which is a valid density","It is $\\tfrac12$ everywhere, by symmetry"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"24ca112dabfa2e82853c9c8f","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For $x,y\\ge0$, a pair has joint CDF $F(x,y)=1-\\mathrm{e}^{-x}-\\mathrm{e}^{-y}+\\mathrm{e}^{-x-y-xy}$. What is its joint density at $(1,1)$?","options":["$\\mathrm{e}^{-3}$","$4\\mathrm{e}^{-3}$","$3\\mathrm{e}^{-3}$","$\\mathrm{e}^{-2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7348a3e4cf5e0dbe30b7b02f","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_joint_cdf_continuous_6433278e_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"$X$ and $Y$ are independent $\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$. What is the joint CDF of $(X,\\,X+Y)$ at $(0.5,1)$?","options":["$0.25$","$0.5$","$0.125$","$0.375$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d205b2239480234a9caade0f","time_limit":40}]}}